  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Determine the discriminant $\\Delta$, then solve $\\Delta=0$ to find the values of $g$ for which each quadratic function has exactly one zero.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=gx^2-8x+g$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\Delta=64-4g^2$; $g=-4$ or $g=4$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-8)^2-4(g)(g)\\\\\\Delta&=64-4g^2\\end{align*}$$\nExactly one zero requires $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}64-4g^2&=0\\\\g^2&=16\\\\g&=-4\\quad\\text{or}\\quad g=4\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values give a non-zero coefficient of $x^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=4x^2+4gx+(6g+7)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\Delta=16(g^2-6g-7)$; $g=-1$ or $g=7$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(4g)^2-4(4)(6g+7)\\\\\\Delta&=16g^2-96g-112\\\\\\Delta&=16(g^2-6g-7)\\end{align*}$$\nExactly one zero requires $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}16(g^2-6g-7)&=0\\\\g^2-6g-7&=0\\\\(g-7)(g+1)&=0\\\\g&=7\\quad\\text{or}\\quad g=-1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=9x^2-2(g+1)x+1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\Delta=4(g^2+2g-8)$; $g=-4$ or $g=2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=[-2(g+1)]^2-4(9)(1)\\\\\\Delta&=4(g+1)^2-36\\\\\\Delta&=4(g^2+2g-8)\\end{align*}$$\nExactly one zero requires $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}4(g^2+2g-8)&=0\\\\g^2+2g-8&=0\\\\(g+4)(g-2)&=0\\\\g&=-4\\quad\\text{or}\\quad g=2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$y=(2g-3)x^2+(5g-1)x+(3g+2)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\Delta=(g+5)^2$; $g=-5$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(5g-1)^2-4(2g-3)(3g+2)\\\\\\Delta&=25g^2-10g+1-4(6g^2-5g-6)\\\\\\Delta&=g^2+10g+25\\\\\\Delta&=(g+5)^2\\end{align*}$$\nExactly one zero requires $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(g+5)^2&=0\\\\g&=-5\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $g=-5$, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $-13\\ne0$, so the function remains quadratic.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      }
    ]
  },
